      "id": 1,
      "claim": "A discharge of contractual duties by operation of law occurs when an external legal event makes performance impossible or illegal, regardless of the parties' intent.",
      "evidence": "Restatement (Second) of Contracts §§ 261‑263 (impossibility/frustration) and case law such as Taylor v. Caldwell (1903) treat impossibility as an automatic legal discharge.",
      "weight": 0.25,
      "depends_on": []
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "claim": "Novation requires the mutual assent of all parties and creates a new contract, so it is a discharge by agreement, not by operation of law.",
      "evidence": "Restatement (Second) of Contracts § 89 defines novation as a substitution of a new obligation with the consent of the parties.",
      "weight": 0.15,
      "depends_on": []
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "claim": "Accord and satisfaction is a voluntary settlement in which the parties agree to accept different performance, therefore it discharges the contract by agreement, not by operation of law.",
      "evidence": "Accord and satisfaction is described in Restatement (Second) of Contracts §§ 89‑90 as a consensual modification or termination of obligations.",
      "weight": 0.15,
      "depends_on": []
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "claim": "Anticipatory repudiation is a breach that gives the non‑breaching party the right to treat the contract as breached, but it does not itself constitute a discharge by operation of law.",
      "evidence": "Restatement (Second) of Contracts § 251 allows the innocent party to sue or terminate after repudiation, but the discharge results from the party’s election, not automatically by law.",
      "weight": 0.15,
      "depends_on": []
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "claim": "Impossibility of performance discharges contractual duties automatically by operation of law when performance becomes objectively impossible through no fault of the parties.",
      "evidence": "Restatement (Second) of Contracts § 261; the doctrine of impossibility (e.g., Taylor v. Caldwell) holds that duties are excused and discharged by law when the subject matter is destroyed or performance is rendered impossible.",
      "weight": 0.30,
      "depends_on": [1]
    }
  ],
